#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/i2c.h>
#include <linux/init.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>

module.h: Required for writing kernel modules (defines MODULE_* macros, init/exit handling).
i2c.h: Provides APIs for I²C clients, adapters, and helper functions.
init.h: Defines __init and __exit macros for marking init/cleanup functions.
kernel.h: General kernel utilities (pr_info, error codes, etc.).

Fake registers
#define ENV_COMBO_I2C_ADDR 0x39
static u8 envcombo_regs[256];

0x39 is the I²C address that the real driver (cs-driver.ko) expects.
envcombo_regs is a simple byte array simulating the device’s internal register map.


Read function:
static s32 envcombo_sim_read_byte(struct i2c_client *client, u8 reg)
{
    return envcombo_regs[reg];
}
Pretends to perform an I²C read.
Instead of talking to hardware, it just returns whatever is stored in envcombo_regs[reg].

Write function:
static s32 envcombo_sim_write_byte(struct i2c_client *client, u8 reg, u8 val)
{
    envcombo_regs[reg] = val;
    return 0;
}
Pretends to write to a device register.
Just stores val into the envcombo_regs[] array.
Returns 0 to indicate success.

Dummy driver struct (optional):
static struct i2c_driver envcombo_dummy_driver;

Declares a dummy i2c_driver.
If unused, this causes warnings (can be deleted).

Probe/Remove (optional):
static int envcombo_sim_probe(struct i2c_client *client,
                              const struct i2c_device_id *id)
{
    pr_info("envcombo-sim: device probed\n");
    return 0;
}

static int envcombo_sim_remove(struct i2c_client *client)
{
    pr_info("envcombo-sim: device removed\n");
    return 0;
}
Normally used in real I²C drivers.
Here, since we are just simulating, they don’t do much.

Adapter definition:
static struct i2c_adapter envcombo_adapter = {
    .owner = THIS_MODULE,
    .class = I2C_CLASS_HWMON,
    .algo = &some_algo,
    .name = "envcombo-sim-adapter",
};
Creates a virtual I²C bus.

owner: Prevents module unloading while in use.
class: Type of devices supported (here hwmon = sensors).
algo: Pointer to an I²C algorithm structure (you might stub this).
name: Identifier visible in /sys.

Module init:
static int __init envcombo_sim_init(void)
{
    struct i2c_board_info info = {
        I2C_BOARD_INFO("env-combo", ENV_COMBO_I2C_ADDR),
    };
    struct i2c_client *client;
    int ret;

    ret = i2c_add_adapter(&envcombo_adapter);
    if (ret)
        return ret;

    client = i2c_new_client_device(&envcombo_adapter, &info);
    if (IS_ERR(client)) {
        i2c_del_adapter(&envcombo_adapter);
        return PTR_ERR(client);
    }

    pr_info("envcombo-sim: registered fake env-combo device at 0x%02x\n",
            ENV_COMBO_I2C_ADDR);
    return 0;
}
Defines an I²C device (env-combo, address 0x39).

Registers the fake adapter (i2c_add_adapter).
Attaches the fake device (i2c_new_client_device) to that adapter.
➝ This is the magic point: now the real driver (env_combo.ko) sees "env-combo" at 0x39 and binds.
If success: prints a message.

static void __exit envcombo_sim_exit(void)
{
    i2c_del_adapter(&envcombo_adapter);
    pr_info("envcombo-sim: removed\n");
}
Cleans up: unregisters the fake adapter (which deletes the fake client).

In short:

The simulator pretends to be an I²C device (env-combo@0x39).
It registers a fake I²C adapter and adds a device on that adapter.
The real driver (env_combo.ko) binds because it matches .type = "env-combo".
All I²C reads/writes are just accesses to the envcombo_regs[] array.


1. Role of the simulator (i2c-envcombo-sim.ko)

It calls i2c_new_client_device() (or i2c_new_device() in older kernels).
This function instantiates an I²C device on a bus, with:

.type = "env-combo",
.addr = 0x39

That means: “On this bus, there is a device named env-combo at I²C address 0x39.”
Without this step, the kernel doesn’t know such a device exists.

2. Role of the driver (env_combo.ko)

The driver registers itself as an i2c_driver:
static struct i2c_driver env_combo_driver = {
    .driver = { .name = "env-combo" },
    .probe  = env_combo_probe,
    .id_table = env_combo_id,
};
The I²C core compares the .type string "env-combo" (from the device created by the simulator) with the driver’s .name and .id_table.
If they match → the kernel binds the driver to the device, and env_combo_probe() is called.
